X-Reposting-Policy: redistribute only with permission

Peter C. McCluskey says:
>  esr@snark.thyrsus.com  (Eric S. Raymond) writes:
> >>  Once programming languages and styles adapt to parallelism, the limits
> >> to CPU-speed growth will be economics, not physics.
> >
> >This is by no means clear.  There are many important problems that are
> >intrinsically serial.  No matter how hard you try, for example, you won't
> >get factor K speedup out of splitting a sort between K processors.  The
> >merge of sorted sublists isn't quite O(n**2) but it's not O(n) either.
> >
> >Massive parallelism is a big win for numerical computing, for pattern
> >recognition, and for a number of AI problems that are hard to attack
> >serially.  However, the belief that it's a panacea that will end-run us
> >around quantum speed restrictions is confined to people who haven't done
> >any serious algorithmic analysis.
> 
>  My assertion implicitly included the claim that typical problems which will
> require faster processors can benefit significantly from parallelization.
> I stand by this claim, although I don't claim that the speedup will be
> linear in the number of processors, nor do I mean to trivialize the paradigm
> shift that will be needed.

I must agree with Peter.

Human beings run on the slowest processors around -- a neuron is a
very slowly switching device with a very slow set of I/O channels. Put
enough of them together, and astonishing things can be done.

I can think of dozens of problems in every day computing that
parallelism will do well for.

Mundane things like spreadsheet calculation, running monthly billing
reports, and the like are easily parallelized conceptually. Important
things like routing, which is a nasty problem the solution to which is
needed if the internet is to survive, have been shown to be
parallelizable very effectively. Computer vision, one of the few areas
of AI for which I retain respect, is a subject that has made great
strides thanks to massively parallel computers. Fast parallel graphics
rendering systems are quite easily conceived of and in fact are used
in certain high end applications. 

Most people who say "parallel computers won't be able to do X for me"
are people who barely ever get the CPU on their Sparcstation-2 above
5% utilization. Even I barely ever see my CPU tick over -- all my
every day computing tasks are for practical purposes instantaneous --
running the Perl script that did my income taxes (yes, I have such a
thing!) was so fast that I could barely measure the time it took. On
the other hand, down the hall there are some guys who do REAL
computing -- Collateralized Mortgate Obligation calculations. They eat
CPU like its going out of style -- and they do it by stringing
together a dozen sparcstations and running a fancy parallel calculator
on them all night. Most of the "real" cpu-intensive problems, like
weather prediction or airflow modeling, easily parallelize. Even a
compiler can easily be broken into about four parallel segments if you
do it right -- admittedly not more, but compiling is no longer a
compute intensive task by modern standards (the Plan 9 compilers
recompile the operating system in seconds.)

Anyway, enough on this from me.

Perry


