Date: Thu, 15 Apr 93 16:24:17 PDT
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: TECH: Genetic algorithms question

> From: pavel@PARK.BU.EDU (Paul Cisek)

> My first question is: What determines the dominance of one gene
> over another?  Is it a part of the code in the cistron itself or is
> it a property of the chromosome that it happens to be carried by?
> The simple example I always hear is that blue eye color is
> recessive and brown is dominant.

In GA terms, it's a property of the genotype's expression.  Perhaps
the brown-eye allele codes for an enzyme involved in the production
of brown pigment, whereas the blue-eye allele codes for a broken
version of that protein.  (I'm just postulating this, as an
illustration of a mechanism for recessivity.)  Somebody with one
copy of each allele will have brown eyes.  Even if less "brown
synthase" enzyme is present (which will depend on other factors
which we don't need to discuss), it will be sufficient to produce
the pigment to be deposited.  Perhaps the gray-blue eyes of infancy
will remain a bit longer.

If instead of (hypothetically) coding for a broken enzyme, the
blue-eyes allele coded for some unpleasant protein that caused you
to drop dead at age 50, it would be dominant instead.  Both of these
endpoint situations, as well as the variety of intermediates, could
be implemented in a GA.  You'd need a polyploid genotype,
obviously.  Then you could do your translation to phenotype in such
a way as to produce recessive and dominant behavior, by dealing
differently with differences in the alleles at parallel genes.

(Are there any other examples of a word pair, like "iris" the flower
and "iris" the thing in your eye, in which the words are identical
in the singular but have different plurals?)

> Thus, we can expect a pool of recessive genes to show more variety
> in mutations than the dominant ones do.  When a population
> decreases in size, the likelihood that an organism winds up with
> two recessive genes increases, and thus we expect more mutations
> (as seen in cases of inbreeding).

Recessive alleles will be less subject to evolutionary pressure,
yes.  But the place where this effect really comes into play is in
the introns.  These are not (presently) expressed at all.  They
consist partly of old genetic material, of copies of things that
have been quietly mutating for a few million years.  Occasionally
something in there will get turned on -- and the resulting organism
probably never is born.  But occasionally something useful will turn
up.  This sort of thing could happen with recessive alleles too, I
guess.

I'm not sure what you're saying about population decrease, though.
The likelihood of being a homozygous recessive is a function of the
allele's prevalence, not of population size.  Are you saying that
the population is being selectively culled of carriers of the
dominant allele?  This sort of selective pressure will certainly
cause a shift in frequency.

> My next question is:  Has this kind of mechanism been attempted in
> genetic algorithms?  What kind of results were observed?

Good question.  Anybody?

> -Paul
 
