I criticized what I percieved as hyperbole by Perry Metzger, to which
he responded:

>I think you don't understand the point I am making -- its oblique to
>yours.  ... Never mind all that. The point is this -- no matter how 
>it happens, its going to end up happening extremely fast on a
>reasonable time scale. ... The point of my off-the-cuff speedup
>scenario was not to imply that uploading would happen in one day. 

The problem with writing, as opposed to talking to yourself, is that
you have to deal with a wide range of possible readers, with different
goals and perspectives.  The claims I objected to may not have been
central from your perspective, but they might be central to me.  Wise
folk avoid writing claims they don't believe and which aren't central
to their concerns.  

A similar problem happens when people say "A libertarian government
would eliminate poverty, discrimination, war, ...", and then when
questioned back off to "well, my main point is just that things would
be better".  If that's their main point, best to just start with that.

Perry summarizes:

>Many scenarios are possible. I don't care to speculate what precisely
>will happen. All I know is that at some point within at lowest twenty
>five years and at most about one hundred years, we are going to have
>intelligent entities spawned by our race operating at 10^8 times the
>speed of humans by some reasonable benchmark. When that happens the
>world will become completely incomprehensible to humans. Whether that
>happens slowly or quickly, over a decade or in one day, etc, does not
>matter. What does matter is the predictive brick wall, and that it is
>showing up SOON.

Well axiomatically, there's no point in speculating beyond the horizon
point where we can't predict at all.  And if the pace of social change
and subjective experience for the dominant entities in the economy
speeds up dramatically, then this horizon in time will be fewer
rotations of the earth about the sun away.  These aren't interesting
observations to me.

To me, the interesting thing to note is that we can forsee an upcoming
dramatic transition period, that of uploading.  While we may not be
able to forsee the upload world a million subjective years after it
has begun, so what?  I claim we can forsee into the first one hundred
subjective upload years, and that it is well worth our effort to do
so.  I've posted on this subject many times here, specifically
regarding the economic and social consequences of easy copying.

Robin Hanson

