If you just remove half of the population randomly with respect to
the gene in question, the removed half and the remaining half will
have the same expected allele frequencies, just by symmetry.  You
might expect more inbreeding, but really, those related to you are
as likely to be removed as those not.

Let's say alleles "A" and "a" are equally frequent.  Then a
population of 100 will be 25 aa, 50 Aa, and 25 AA -- 75%
phenotypically A.  Randomly removing half will just make this 12,
25, and 12 -- still 75% A.  And this population is stable under
random mating.

