Subject: COMP: Complexity of sorting

	The reason that  radix sort is able to work in linear time is
that it depends on the number of digits in the number being sorted. So while
it is not (n*lg n), it is (d * lg n), where d is the logarithm of n or larger.
So its not that amazing really. It is however theoretically very very fast,
however some of the niceties make it difficult to implement properly. As I 
recall if you had the special comparison operation required (which most
processors don't) then it can be done in about 3 or 4 lines of assembly for
each comparison, not using multiplies or divides por anything else expensive.
Look into it if you are writing an assembly language sorting routine.
