Date: Fri, 16 Apr 1993 00:34:26 -0400 (EDT)
From: esr@snark.thyrsus.com (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: TECH: Genetic algorithms question

>                   In fact, the blue-eyes allele probably doesn't
> code for a broken brown synthase.  Maybe it codes for blue synthase.
> Somebody probably knows the details, but all of the eyes *my* bio
> classes talked about were attached to fruit flies.

I used to know this.  Lemme se if I can remember the details...

Basic fact: blue human eyes are blue for the same basic reason the sky is
blue; that's the color you get when approximately-white light scatters off
aerosols in a certain size range.

The sky has no diffused blue pigment in it.  What it's got instead is water
vapor, which sunlight scatters off of to produce blue.  (BTW, red skies are
due to dust-sized aerosols; that's why air pollution makes pretty sunsets.)

Blue human eyes have no diffused pigment either (or, very little; there are
lighter and darker shades).  The blue is produced by scattering of light
reflected off the iris.  The scattering is due to pigment granules in the
cornea that are the same size range as water vapor droplets in the sky.

You can check out the no-diffused-pigment part by looking through the cornea
of a blue-eyed person sideways.  It's transparent!   (My girlfriend and I
just checked this.  She has sort of blue/gray eyes with a hazel outer ring;
the cornea is hazel-tinged.  I have your classic clear blue eyes and my cornea
is, in fact, transparent.)

Other eye colors have diffuse pigment in the cornea.  This is easiest to see
in brown-eyed people.

Incidentally, blue eyes are adaptive in a way that explains their correlation
with Northern European ancestry.  Blue-eyed people have slightly better seeing
in foggy and misty conditions.  It's not known for sure what the *presence*
of eye pigment is good for, though I've seen suggestions that it may help
protect the eyes from UV damage.
-- 
