Date: Fri, 16 Apr 93 09:37:19 BST
From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
Subject: Korzybski's "E-Prime", also NLP

Here are some General Semantics references.

Korzybski "Science and Sanity" (preferably the latest (4th) edition, as
later editions include extensive extra prefaces and supplements)

S.I. Hayakawa "Language in Thought and Action" (I read this a long time
ago; I recall that it was written on a very elementary level.)

Stuart Chase "The Tyranny of Words" and "The Power of Words"

D.D. Bourland and P.D. Johnston (eds.) "To Be Or Not" (a collection of
articles on E-Prime)

The Korzybski and Bourland books are available from the International
Society for General Semantics, P.O. Box 2469, San Francisco, CA 94126, tel.
(415) 543-1747.  The ISGS also publishes a quarterly journal on GS called
"Etc", and have published various other books on GS.

E-Prime has received a great deal of criticism, much of which I feel is
misplaced.  Certainly, it is possible to write E-Prime following the letter
of the rules but missing the point of the exercise or wilfully ignoring it;
well, anything can be caricatured, regardless of its merits.  Caricature is
a way of presenting a point of view, not an argument that the point of view
has any support.

I personally treat writing in E-Prime in the same spirit as creative
writing exercises.  A typical such exercise consists of choosing half a
dozen words at random and writing a sensible paragraph using all of them. 
Someone who responds to such an exercise by writing a paragraph of the form
"I opened a dictionary at random and the first six words I found were..."
has missed the point and will learn nothing from it.  Similarly, writing
E-Prime by means of fractured English in which all uses of "to be" simply
omitted also missing the point.

I do find that since learning of E-Prime and of GS in general, that a
warning flag goes up in my mind whenever I encounter excessively izzy
statements or questions such as "what *is* X, *really*?".  Words like
"really", "essentially", "only", etc. have the same effect.  They usually
disguise muddled thinking.  I even regard the word "the" with suspicion on
occasion.

